    - target: {fileID: 6163876341813508865, guid: 32cf0b5af8e50674fa364e0526edb2dc, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Text
      value: "The Oak Toad (Anaxyrus quercicus) can be found quite commonly throughout
        Florida, except for the Florida Keys. They are a smaller species of Toad
        which comes in at a tiny .75\u201D to 1.5\u201D in length. They make their
        homes among the sandy soils by costal areas. They can be distinguished by
        their signature bright white, yellowish, or orange stripe that runs from
        its snout to its tail. Common colors for them are brown, grey, or almost
        black, often with scattered reddish warts and mottled black spots across
        their back in sets of 3 or 5. Their diet consists mainly of insects such
        as beetles, centipedes, spiders, and other invertebrates; although they mainly
        prefer ants. They are nocturnal creatures spending most of their time chirping
        away as their ovular vocal sacs inflate, usually serving an indication of
        breeding season."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
